{"name":"Fudge","hex":"#583d43","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Fudge","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Fudge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fudge","blocks":[{"text":"Fudge is a confection made by mixing sugar, butter, and milk. Modern fudge has its origins in the 19th century United States, and became popular in American women's colleges late in the century. Fudge can come in a variety of flavorings depending on the region or country it was made; popular flavors include fruit, nut, chocolate, and caramel. Fudge is often bought as a gift from a gift shop in tourist areas and attractions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Handmade_fruit_fudge_squares.jpg/330px-Handmade_fruit_fudge_squares.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fudge","wikidata":"Q379138"},{"title":"Fudge (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Fudge is a 1995 American children's television series based on a series of Judy Blume novels about a young boy nicknamed Fudge. The series ran for two seasons, with 24 episodes following a telefilm adaptation of Blume's novel Fudge-a-Mania, which aired on January 7, 1995, in primetime, as part of the ABC Family Movies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Fudge_%28Amblin_TV_series%29_logo.svg/330px-Fudge_%28Amblin_TV_series%29_logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fudge_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q5507059"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Karo_Corn_Syrup_fudge_advertisement.png/500px-Karo_Corn_Syrup_fudge_advertisement.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Karo_Corn_Syrup_fudge_advertisement.png/500px-Karo_Corn